WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.810 --> 00:00:06.790
So last week in our staff meeting the conversation
came about after we were reading the book,

00:00:06.790 --> 00:00:08.900
Contagious, and I asked the question from
everybody.

00:00:08.900 --> 00:00:15.180
I said, hey, can you tell me why I make it
a requirement for us to read a book a month?

00:00:15.180 --> 00:00:17.180
We've been doing this for seven-and-a-half
years.

00:00:17.180 --> 00:00:18.180
Why?

00:00:18.180 --> 00:00:19.180
And I got four responses.

00:00:19.180 --> 00:00:21.369
One of them said, Pat, you want us to improve.

00:00:21.369 --> 00:00:22.480
I said, okay, great.

00:00:22.480 --> 00:00:23.480
What else?

00:00:23.480 --> 00:00:28.660
The other person said, you want our minds
to be working and thinking about work after

00:00:28.660 --> 00:00:29.660
hours.

00:00:29.660 --> 00:00:30.759
That was an interesting one.

00:00:30.759 --> 00:00:33.190
Another person said, it brings us together.

00:00:33.190 --> 00:00:35.050
Because we're advancing together.

00:00:35.050 --> 00:00:36.160
Another person said ideas.

00:00:36.160 --> 00:00:37.730
And I said, "Everything is great.

00:00:37.730 --> 00:00:39.290
But let me tell you why we read books."

00:00:39.290 --> 00:00:43.090
I said, "I want all of you to want to make
more money.

00:00:43.090 --> 00:00:44.390
How many of you guys want to make more money?"

00:00:44.390 --> 00:00:45.390
And they raised their hands.

00:00:45.390 --> 00:00:46.610
Now remember, this is not sales.

00:00:46.610 --> 00:00:48.900
This is W-2 jobs.

00:00:48.900 --> 00:00:52.940
How many of you want to ask for a raise and
to get a raise?

00:00:52.940 --> 00:00:54.420
And everyone said, "I want to get a raise."

00:00:54.420 --> 00:00:57.530
Now this is me, teaching my staff how to ask
for a raise.

00:00:57.530 --> 00:01:02.260
I said, "There's three things that you need
to be doing to constantly be advancing in

00:01:02.260 --> 00:01:03.350
your career.

00:01:03.350 --> 00:01:05.870
And if you don't, you're not going to advance
in your career."

00:01:05.870 --> 00:01:06.870
They said, "What is it?"

00:01:06.870 --> 00:01:09.340
And I wrote it out, so I'm sharing that with
you.

00:01:09.340 --> 00:01:12.120
One is attitude, effort, and progress.

00:01:12.120 --> 00:01:13.600
Let me explain.

00:01:13.600 --> 00:01:15.000
Attitude.

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:18.869
You'll have a lot of talented people in your
career that you'll hire.

00:01:18.869 --> 00:01:25.240
I've hired so many incredibly ridiculous talented
people with bad attitudes.

00:01:25.240 --> 00:01:26.840
And they don't last.

00:01:26.840 --> 00:01:28.670
And they don't last anywhere they go.

00:01:28.670 --> 00:01:30.469
Because anywhere they go, nobody likes them.

00:01:30.469 --> 00:01:32.310
No one likes a bad attitude.

00:01:32.310 --> 00:01:37.840
So when it comes down to attitude, a saving
grace for a person not to get fired, is a

00:01:37.840 --> 00:01:38.840
positive attitude.

00:01:38.840 --> 00:01:40.479
Because you've got three opportunities with
attitude.

00:01:40.479 --> 00:01:44.719
One is a person that comes in, they're not
positive, they're not negative, it's just

00:01:44.719 --> 00:01:47.229
no one knows they're around.

00:01:47.229 --> 00:01:48.229
Nobody does.

00:01:48.229 --> 00:01:53.329
You can walk by their cubicle 50 times and
they get fired one day, and you don't even

00:01:53.329 --> 00:01:54.329
know they got fired.

00:01:54.329 --> 00:01:55.619
Because no one knows their attitude.

00:01:55.619 --> 00:01:56.770
Because you never see them.

00:01:56.770 --> 00:01:58.189
Because they don't say anything.

00:01:58.189 --> 00:01:59.889
So there's not a positivity or negativity.

00:01:59.889 --> 00:02:02.909
At least you remember the negative person,
and you remember the positive person.

00:02:02.909 --> 00:02:05.810
But you don't remember the person in the neutral,
right?

00:02:05.810 --> 00:02:07.840
Negative ones, eventually, they're contagious.

00:02:07.840 --> 00:02:12.590
Anytime I look at a resume somebody gives
you a resume to hire, I look at how many jobs

00:02:12.590 --> 00:02:15.209
this person had the last two, three, four,
five years.

00:02:15.209 --> 00:02:19.819
If it's multiple jobs, one after another after
another after another, there is something

00:02:19.819 --> 00:02:20.819
there.

00:02:20.819 --> 00:02:22.519
This can't constantly be the company.

00:02:22.519 --> 00:02:24.040
Companies don't let go of good people.

00:02:24.040 --> 00:02:25.040
They just don't.

00:02:25.040 --> 00:02:26.040
They want to keep good people.

00:02:26.040 --> 00:02:29.870
There's something that didn't work out six
different times, and most of the time it comes

00:02:29.870 --> 00:02:31.250
down to attitude.

00:02:31.250 --> 00:02:32.360
So then positive attitude.

00:02:32.360 --> 00:02:35.579
We had an employee in the room and I said,
"Hey, let me talk to you guys about her."

00:02:35.579 --> 00:02:36.579
I pointed out her.

00:02:36.579 --> 00:02:38.780
And I said, "You know you almost got fired
six months ago, four months ago."

00:02:38.780 --> 00:02:39.780
She said, "Yeah, I know."

00:02:39.780 --> 00:02:40.780
I said, "How did you know?"

00:02:40.780 --> 00:02:42.030
She said, "Everybody told me I'm about to
get fired."

00:02:42.030 --> 00:02:43.860
I said, "Do you know why you didn't get fired?"

00:02:43.860 --> 00:02:45.159
She said, "Why?"

00:02:45.159 --> 00:02:48.370
So I had an executive meeting, meeting with
the executives, and I said, "This person needs

00:02:48.370 --> 00:02:50.430
to get fired, that person needs to get fired,
let's keep these three.

00:02:50.430 --> 00:02:51.939
Let's give a raise to this person," etc. etc.

00:02:51.939 --> 00:02:53.340
We have these meetings.

00:02:53.340 --> 00:02:57.019
I said, "You were on the verge of getting
fired because you had no clue what you were

00:02:57.019 --> 00:02:58.250
doing with your job."

00:02:58.250 --> 00:02:59.650
And she said, "Yeah, honestly, I had no idea."

00:02:59.650 --> 00:03:06.040
I said, "But the only reason why we didn't
fire you, is because you have such a positive

00:03:06.040 --> 00:03:07.409
attitude.

00:03:07.409 --> 00:03:09.640
You're contagious to be around.

00:03:09.640 --> 00:03:10.640
That's your saving grace.

00:03:10.640 --> 00:03:12.610
You have no idea how valuable that is."

00:03:12.610 --> 00:03:14.670
And she's like, "Oh my gosh, this is so amazing."

00:03:14.670 --> 00:03:17.549
I said, "You stay like that, and you're going
to do great things with your life."

00:03:17.549 --> 00:03:18.549
The next one is effort.

00:03:18.549 --> 00:03:19.980
Let me explain to you the effort.

00:03:19.980 --> 00:03:21.659
You know who the guys in the middle are?

00:03:21.659 --> 00:03:24.060
I call them 7:59 to 5:01.

00:03:24.060 --> 00:03:28.340
They clock in the minute before, and they
clock out the minute after.

00:03:28.340 --> 00:03:30.269
I've got to go watch my T.V.

00:03:30.269 --> 00:03:31.269
shows.

00:03:31.269 --> 00:03:34.610
And there's no way in the world I'm working
one minute more for this corporation than

00:03:34.610 --> 00:03:35.610
I'm supposed.

00:03:35.610 --> 00:03:36.610
I'm not doing that.

00:03:36.610 --> 00:03:37.610
I'm just not doing that.

00:03:37.610 --> 00:03:38.610
Right?

00:03:38.610 --> 00:03:39.610
It's like a religion to them.

00:03:39.610 --> 00:03:41.110
Still cool, you're not negative.

00:03:41.110 --> 00:03:43.100
Because these guys on the bottom, you know
what they do?

00:03:43.100 --> 00:03:44.950
Let me tell you about their effort.

00:03:44.950 --> 00:03:48.560
These guys for whatever reason, they're sick
on Mondays and they don't feel too well on

00:03:48.560 --> 00:03:49.680
Friday afternoons.

00:03:49.680 --> 00:03:50.680
I don't know what it is.

00:03:50.680 --> 00:03:54.480
Their timing is incredible on when they don't
feel well.

00:03:54.480 --> 00:03:55.480
Right?

00:03:55.480 --> 00:03:59.680
Now I've worked jobs before and I know why
on Mondays because you want to have a longer

00:03:59.680 --> 00:04:03.530
weekend and I know why Fridays because you
want to have a longer weekend, but eventually

00:04:03.530 --> 00:04:04.670
people figure that part out.

00:04:04.670 --> 00:04:06.749
I mean, everyone knows what's going on there.

00:04:06.749 --> 00:04:08.870
These guys [positive] always move up.

00:04:08.870 --> 00:04:17.049
I remember working at many of my jobs that
it goes back to, if we had to do certain things

00:04:17.049 --> 00:04:22.120
at Bally's and I was off at 8:00 or 9:00 or
10:00, I was there till midnight.

00:04:22.120 --> 00:04:24.110
I didn't have to be there until midnight.

00:04:24.110 --> 00:04:26.110
I didn't get paid those hours.

00:04:26.110 --> 00:04:27.500
No one paid me for those hours.

00:04:27.500 --> 00:04:28.830
But I stayed till midnight.

00:04:28.830 --> 00:04:30.630
Now what happened when I stayed there till
midnight?

00:04:30.630 --> 00:04:34.889
One, I grew for myself, but people started
saying, "Pat's one of the hardest working

00:04:34.889 --> 00:04:36.250
guys in the company."

00:04:36.250 --> 00:04:37.660
I became the rookie of the year.

00:04:37.660 --> 00:04:38.660
I got recognition.

00:04:38.660 --> 00:04:40.420
I got those accolades, but you know what else
happened?

00:04:40.420 --> 00:04:41.940
Let me tell you what else happened.

00:04:41.940 --> 00:04:46.780
It prepped me that one day if I was ever going
to be put into an executive position, I already

00:04:46.780 --> 00:04:47.789
know about the effort part.

00:04:47.789 --> 00:04:49.310
I'm not afraid of working hard.

00:04:49.310 --> 00:04:51.930
I don't mind putting in 12, 13, 14, 16 hour
days.

00:04:51.930 --> 00:04:52.930
Because I've done it.

00:04:52.930 --> 00:04:54.169
It's a muscle now.

00:04:54.169 --> 00:04:56.949
I'm always going to be needed because most
people don't work that hard.

00:04:56.949 --> 00:05:00.710
So for you watching this, I promise you, these
two [attitude and effort] alone, you're going

00:05:00.710 --> 00:05:02.100
to move up in your career.

00:05:02.100 --> 00:05:03.220
I promise you that.

00:05:03.220 --> 00:05:07.931
Because most people don't work too hard, and
most people don't consistently have a positive

00:05:07.931 --> 00:05:08.931
attitude.

00:05:08.931 --> 00:05:10.620
These two, you'll learn the other parts.

00:05:10.620 --> 00:05:11.870
Now let's get to the last one.

00:05:11.870 --> 00:05:14.020
The last one is progress.

00:05:14.020 --> 00:05:16.430
Progress to me is I call a meeting.

00:05:16.430 --> 00:05:19.509
And this meeting, I say, "These five people
I want to be part of this meeting.

00:05:19.509 --> 00:05:24.039
We're going to do an ideation meeting on a
new software, on a new technology, on a new

00:05:24.039 --> 00:05:27.169
system, on a new product, on a new show, on
whatever."

00:05:27.169 --> 00:05:33.289
I sit there, as the person who called the
meeting, and I watch on who's giving feedback.

00:05:33.289 --> 00:05:36.800
The person that doesn't give feedback, I don't
invite to the next meeting.

00:05:36.800 --> 00:05:37.800
For what?

00:05:37.800 --> 00:05:38.800
You don't give feedback.

00:05:38.800 --> 00:05:39.800
What's the purpose of you sitting there?

00:05:39.800 --> 00:05:40.800
To look pretty?

00:05:40.800 --> 00:05:42.610
I mean, listen, we don't get paid to model
here, right?

00:05:42.610 --> 00:05:44.490
We get paid to come up with ideas.

00:05:44.490 --> 00:05:45.490
Okay.

00:05:45.490 --> 00:05:46.490
Cool.

00:05:46.490 --> 00:05:54.319
The person that constantly comes up with ideas
and they don't get - what's the attitude - when

00:05:54.319 --> 00:05:58.069
somebody gets ideas shut down and they become
defensive and they - they don't freeze.

00:05:58.069 --> 00:06:01.250
When you say, "No, we can't do the idea right
now."

00:06:01.250 --> 00:06:02.460
And they go like this [arms crossed].

00:06:02.460 --> 00:06:04.030
Because you're too sensitive now.

00:06:04.030 --> 00:06:05.789
Instead, this person says, "Okay, cool.

00:06:05.789 --> 00:06:06.789
No problem."

00:06:06.789 --> 00:06:07.789
Boom.

00:06:07.789 --> 00:06:08.789
Next.

00:06:08.789 --> 00:06:09.789
Because sometimes some ideas are just not
workable.

00:06:09.789 --> 00:06:13.789
And they keep feeding more and feeding more,
and man, this dude's got a great attitude,

00:06:13.789 --> 00:06:18.569
he's so enthusiastic about being here and
he's working hard, and he's giving great ideas,

00:06:18.569 --> 00:06:19.720
trifecta, I like this guy.

00:06:19.720 --> 00:06:21.160
Let's keep him long term.

00:06:21.160 --> 00:06:24.760
Let's give him more responsibility and move
him up in the company and give him a raise.

00:06:24.760 --> 00:06:26.099
That's the progress.

00:06:26.099 --> 00:06:27.620
How can I make me better?

00:06:27.620 --> 00:06:29.160
How can I make the company better?

00:06:29.160 --> 00:06:30.629
How can I make the industry better?

00:06:30.629 --> 00:06:32.020
How can I make this thing grow faster?

00:06:32.020 --> 00:06:33.599
How can I make this thing look better?

00:06:33.599 --> 00:06:35.389
How can we make the process safer?

00:06:35.389 --> 00:06:37.160
How can we make this a funner place?

00:06:37.160 --> 00:06:38.530
How can we grow better?

00:06:38.530 --> 00:06:41.169
Everything's about better, better, better,
better.

00:06:41.169 --> 00:06:43.510
And the one on the bottom is the one that
walks around and says, Oh my gosh.

00:06:43.510 --> 00:06:44.510
I hate working here.

00:06:44.510 --> 00:06:45.510
Did you hear what he said?

00:06:45.510 --> 00:06:46.510
Here he goes again.

00:06:46.510 --> 00:06:47.510
Did you hear what he said?

00:06:47.510 --> 00:06:48.510
Oh, here he goes.

00:06:48.510 --> 00:06:49.510
You know none of this stuff works.

00:06:49.510 --> 00:06:50.510
Bitching, bitching, bitching, bitching.

00:06:50.510 --> 00:06:54.259
These two (bad attitude, bad progress) become
best friends.

00:06:54.259 --> 00:06:55.530
They're typically the same person.

00:06:55.530 --> 00:06:58.199
Somehow, some way, they join the same club.

00:06:58.199 --> 00:06:59.199
I don't know what it is.

00:06:59.199 --> 00:07:00.940
They attract each other.

00:07:00.940 --> 00:07:04.990
But these guys (good attitude, good progress)
somehow, some way, always move up.

00:07:04.990 --> 00:07:06.410
I can't tell you why it is.

00:07:06.410 --> 00:07:08.050
It's just what it is.

00:07:08.050 --> 00:07:13.169
So if you're watching this right now, whether
you're an entrepreneur, whether you're a businessman,

00:07:13.169 --> 00:07:16.780
whether you're successful already and an executive,
whether you're running a company that did

00:07:16.780 --> 00:07:21.640
$180 million last year in revenue and you're
feeling like it's great, always go back and

00:07:21.640 --> 00:07:24.860
take an inventory of yourself and the people
on your team.

00:07:24.860 --> 00:07:27.950
How's the ideation of you constantly figuring
out how to make things better [progress],

00:07:27.950 --> 00:07:31.050
how is your effort being put in [effort],
how's your attitude?

00:07:31.050 --> 00:07:34.620
Take the assessment of your people and the
people that are there [positive], help them

00:07:34.620 --> 00:07:38.250
move up and if you're somebody that's not
doing these three, try to become a trifecta.

00:07:38.250 --> 00:07:40.540
Ask yourself every day, "How's my attitude
today?

00:07:40.540 --> 00:07:41.540
How's my effort today?

00:07:41.540 --> 00:07:44.949
How am I becoming better at making ideas and
progressing the company?"

00:07:44.949 --> 00:07:50.810
And the more you do that, if the company you're
working at right now, treats you like crap,

00:07:50.810 --> 00:07:55.319
and I'm talking sincerely, not you watching,
yeah, that's why I'm not moving up in life

00:07:55.319 --> 00:08:00.039
- but you're really lazy - I'm talking you've
got a great positive attitude regularly, you

00:08:00.039 --> 00:08:03.020
regularly work hard, and you always come up
with ideas and they still don't treat you

00:08:03.020 --> 00:08:06.419
well, eventually, someone's going to take
notice.

00:08:06.419 --> 00:08:07.500
That I promise you.

00:08:07.500 --> 00:08:09.099
Eventually, someone's going to take notice.

00:08:09.099 --> 00:08:10.110
You're going to move up.

00:08:10.110 --> 00:08:11.110
It's inevitable.

00:08:11.110 --> 00:08:12.110
It's a law.

00:08:12.110 --> 00:08:13.110
It works for everybody.

00:08:13.110 --> 00:08:15.539
So just focus on these three areas.

00:08:15.539 --> 00:08:17.570
Mario, let's get our favorite pillow.

00:08:17.570 --> 00:08:21.080
Okay, today we have Mario throwing us the
pillow instead of Paul.

00:08:21.080 --> 00:08:23.689
We got two new team members here behind us.

00:08:23.689 --> 00:08:25.919
One likes - you said comedy?

00:08:25.919 --> 00:08:27.160
He like comedy.

00:08:27.160 --> 00:08:31.310
And this one wants to be a producer is what
he wants to be.

00:08:31.310 --> 00:08:38.820
And we just talked about the question why
Dane Cook is no longer, he hasn't been on

00:08:38.820 --> 00:08:40.289
the scene for awhile.

00:08:40.289 --> 00:08:43.229
Mario gave us some feedback why, that some
of you guys may remember Dane Cook.

00:08:43.229 --> 00:08:44.229
Some of you don't.

00:08:44.229 --> 00:08:48.760
But regardless, if you haven't yet subscribed
to this channel, let's see if I'm doing this

00:08:48.760 --> 00:08:49.760
right.

00:08:49.760 --> 00:08:50.760
Am I doing it right?

00:08:50.760 --> 00:08:51.760
Is this good here?

00:08:51.760 --> 00:08:53.829
The guys are trying to critique me on where
to put my finger.

